nd trace would show it. Unless... the \"goomba\" drawn is not this. Hmm.\n\nWait, perhaps my trace ran while no enemy was visible: after resume, the Goomba might have collided with Mario during the trace and despawned, or the game state changed. But 0 accesses over 90 frames is suspicious. Let me check current screen / list_sprites.\n\nActually, maybe the game was paused by trace? No.\n\nLet me re-examine: perhaps reads of PRG ROM are not tracked by Mesen's memory callbacks in the same way; trace_writes on prgRom reads may not trigger for ROM reads? The tool documents trace_writes on CPU addresses including PRG reads (the mapping's examples traced read on $A690, $9F01, $8CEF — those are PRG ROM CPU addresses). So it works.\n\nSo $E792 is genuinely not read. Therefore the Goomba's tiles are NOT read from 0x6792 at draw time. Then what is that table? It might be a table read at a different time, or... maybe it's the ending sequence table, or the table is used by a routine only for some enemies.\n\nHold on. Maybe the Goomba's OAM tiles come from a table indexed by enemy type at a different base, and the 0x6792 entry is a *duplicate* used elsewhere (e.g., \"enemy graphics\" for the title screen or for the flag). But search for \"FC FC 70 71 72 73\" was unique in PRG. So if the game ever draws the Goomba with those tiles from ROM, it reads this. Unless it uses a table of pointers and the entry it reads is elsewhere but with same values... no, unique.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the reads happen but trace_writes only counts reads at exact addresses in the range and the code reads with an offset that goes $E792... yes that's in range.\n\nWait — maybe the read happens during NMI, and trace's callback counts only during the frames after install. It installed and ran 90 frames. Should catch.\n\nUnless the game state during those 90 frames had no Goomba (it died?). Let me check screen now.\n\nLet me screenshot + list_sprites, and verify mapping prgRom vs cpuDebug at 0xE792."}
