,252, 252,150,151,152,153,252, 252,...\nIf entries are 6 bytes aligned to 0x6750 (as established), then 0x6740-0x6749 is part of an entry ending at 0x674F: e.g. entry at 0x6744: FC 69 A5 6A A7 A8? Hmm.\n\nWait, from 0x6740 read (48 bytes = 0x6740..0x676F):\nidx0 170 AA\n1 171 AB\n2 172 AC\n3 173 AD\n4 252 FC\n5 252 FC\n6 174 AE\n7 175 AF\n8 176 B0\n9 177 B1\n10 252 FC\n11 165 A5\n12 166 A6\n13 167 A7\n14 168 A8\n15 169 A9\n16 252 FC\n17 160 A0\n18 161 A1\n19 162 A2\n20 163 A3\n21 164 A4\n22 105 69\n23 165 A5\n24 106 6A\n25 167 A7\n26 168 A8\n27 169 A9\n28 107 6B\n29 160 A0\n30 108 6C\n31 162 A2\n32 163 A3\n33 164 A4\n34 252 FC\n35 252 FC\n36 150 96\n37 151 97\n38 152 98\n39 153 99\n40 252 FC\n41 252 FC\n42 154 9A\n43 155 9B\n44 156 9C\n45 157 9D\n46 252 FC\n47 252 FC\n\nSo 0x6740..0x6745 = AA AB AC AD FC FC? Hmm index0-5 = AA AB AC AD FC FC. Then 0x6746-0x674B = AE AF B0 B1 FC A5. Then 0x674C = A6 A7 A8 A9 FC A0... \n\nThat doesn't align to 6-byte entries starting at 0x6750 cleanly. Let's check 6-byte entries starting at 0x6744: bytes 4-9 = FC FC AE AF B0 B1 → an entry! Then 0x674A: bytes 10-15 = FC A5 A6 A7 A8 A9 → hmm starts with one FC. Then 0x6750: bytes 16-21 = FC A0 A1 A2 A3 A4 → one FC. Then 0x6756: FC 69 A5 6A A7 A8? bytes 22-27 = 69 A5 6A A7 A8 A9 — no FC.\n\nHmm, inconsistent. Maybe the grid isn't 6 bytes for all; or my byte offsets from the two different reads are confusing.\n\nLet me be careful: read at 26432 = 0x6740, 48 bytes → covers 0x6740-0x676F.\nread at 26448 = 0x6750, 96 bytes → covers 0x6750-0x67AF.\nThese overlap 0x6750-0x676F; both should agree.\n\nFrom 0x6750 read: bytes = [252,160,161,162,163,164,105,165,106,167,168,169,107,160,108,162,163,164,252,252,150,151,152,153,252,252,154,155,156,157,252,252,143,142,142,143,252,252,149,148,148,149,252,252,220,220,223,223,220,220,221,221,222,222,252,252,178,179,180,181,252,252,182,179,183,181,252,252,112,113,114,115,...]\n\nSo 0x6750: FC A0 A1 A2 A3 A4\n0x6756: 69 A5 6A A7 A8 A9\n0x675C: 6B A0 6C A2 A3